export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>The Construction of 3D Graphics</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>All 3D models begin life as triangles in a 3D modelling application, Maya or Blender or Zbrush. But their collective destination is a display that is plugged to a game console. So, there has to be a process in place to bring our 3D assets onto a 2D plane. Let’s start uncovering that process with polygons.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5752c3d76b5de2bde860.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5752c3d76b5de2bde860.webp\" width=\"553\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d288d5b2ef5fe2f76061.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d288d5b2ef5fe2f76061.webp\" width=\"567\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Polygon</strong></p>\n<p>In the early days, video games were rendered using 2D sprites. But when the industry stepped into the 3D realm, polygons became the standard.  For that reason, every 3D game model is now a collection of many interconnected triangles.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Why triangles?</strong></p>\n<p>A point gives us a position in space. Two connected points give us a line. Three lines give us a plane—a triangle.</p>\n<p>Out of the polygons, the triangle requires the least amount of points, or vertices, to form a surface. It is the most efficient shape performance-wise.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b5c1cd1c7449e27ceced.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b5c1cd1c7449e27ceced.webp\" width=\"494\" height=\"593\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8ef6175065ebd125b379.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8ef6175065ebd125b379.webp\" width=\"582\" height=\"491\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Vertices is the plural form of vertex</strong></p>\n<p>In this picture, we have a 3D coordinate system for measuring length, width and height.</p>\n<p>As you can see, each vertex of the triangle has a set of 3 numbers. These numbers dictate <strong>the position of each vertex</strong> relative to the origin within a space.</p>\n<p>Once all vertices have acquired their coordinate numbers, they will form a triangular plane.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>But it’s not really 3D</strong></p>\n<p>When you create a triangle mesh, you work in a virtual 3D space. But, when your mesh is displayed in-game, it has to be projected on a 2D plane (that is your screen).</p>\n<p>The common projecting method for 3D is called “wireframe rendering.” Basically, it is like shining a flashlight on a hollow cube. The cast shadow will appear 3D even though the wall is totally flat.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ffc6f6067d8a4ec1279b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ffc6f6067d8a4ec1279b.webp\" width=\"406\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e69f9be5689b67d2c1d0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e69f9be5689b67d2c1d0.webp\" width=\"518\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Perspective</strong></p>\n<p>However, if the game console simply projects images like casting shadows, the result will be orthographic, which lacks depth.</p>\n<p>Thus, there will be some extra steps to change it to the <strong>perspective projection</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Pixel</strong></p>\n<p>The word pixel is the combination of picture and element. Pixels take the form of a square.</p>\n<p>The computer screen contains thousands of pixels, and each pixel can display one color from the <strong>R</strong><strong>G</strong><strong>B</strong> color gamut—a range of color that the screen can reproduce using <strong>r</strong>ed, <strong>g</strong>reen, and <strong>b</strong>lue.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/21d93b3ab965cb9a8ae5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/21d93b3ab965cb9a8ae5.webp\" width=\"597\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/876a55550f1e433e45ed.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/876a55550f1e433e45ed.webp\" width=\"536\" height=\"606\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Resolution</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Screen resolution</strong></p>\n<p>Screen resolution refers to the number of pixels a monitor can display, horizontally and vertically.</p>\n<p>For example,  a 1920 x 1080 monitor has 1920 pixels horizontally and 1080 pixels vertically. This number is fixed to each monitor.</p>\n<p>The more pixels a screen has, the sharper the image quality becomes.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Application resolution</strong></p>\n<p>Application resolution refers to the number of pixels an application (an picture, a video or a game) has.</p>\n<p>For example, a 1080p game can still run normally on a 4K screen. However, a 1080p screen cannot display true 4K images.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2b9d5d3697a1c5b78fac.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2b9d5d3697a1c5b78fac.webp\" width=\"571\" height=\"598\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a3262e9b88112c02ccd2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a3262e9b88112c02ccd2.webp\" width=\"483\" height=\"565\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p></td><td><p><strong>EXTRA – </strong><strong>Anti-aliasing</strong></p>\n<p>Since digital images are made of colored squares, they will look jagged when rendering diagonal lines.</p>\n<p>This could be avoided by increasing the resolution—adding more pixels to smooth out the edges. Or, we can turn on different anti-aliasing options in a game. These are techniques that help combat jagged images.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>High dynamic range</strong></p>\n<p><strong>What is it?</strong></p>\n<p>A dynamic range is the ratio between the darkest and lightest values a pixel can assume. By that definition, high dynamic range (HDR) has <strong>darker blacks </strong>and <strong>brighter whites</strong> in comparison to standard dynamic range (SDR). Thanks to the wider gap between black and white, HDR can display<strong> more colors </strong>and create <strong>better contrast</strong>.</p>\n<p>HDR is a set of techniques originating in photography. The basic principle is to have the camera capture multiple pictures at various exposure levels in a quick succession, then blend them together, picking the darkest blacks and brightest whites to present a more accurate depiction of the world as seen through the human eyes.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/57d26788ef5671addcd7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/57d26788ef5671addcd7.webp\" width=\"463\" height=\"596\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/24c5c46bd555285ce849.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/24c5c46bd555285ce849.webp\" width=\"522\" height=\"294\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a>Battlefield 1</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6f8ce5e2801952d1e7a1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6f8ce5e2801952d1e7a1.webp\" width=\"522\" height=\"294\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>How does it affect games?</strong></p>\n<p>HDR works similarly in games, but the implementation requires a lot of color configuration behind the scenes. Since HDR brightens the whites, it could result in <strong>bloom</strong> as seen in the <em>Battlefield 1</em> picture, which can be distracting to players. As for the darkened blacks, while they look great, they could accidentally hide some details in the image (look at the gun).</p>\n<p>Because of these reasons, game studios have to implement HDR on a <strong>game-by-game</strong> basis. That said, the number of HDR-compatible games is increasing quickly.</p>\n<p>Besides, this technology also enhances physically-based rendering (PBR) materials, which we will learn about later.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Rasterization</strong></p>\n<p>Now that we know what triangles and pixels are, let’s look into the conversion. Rasterization is the process of turning vector graphics to raster graphics, turning lines and points into pixels. First, the GPU will determine the highest and lowest vertices of a triangle. Then, it scans from top to bottom, line by line. After scanning, each pixel will be given a color to display the triangle.</p>\n<p>When multiple objects overlap, the GPU will make a list to determine which object is in front and which is in the back. Of course, the real process is more complicated, but this is the gist of it.<br></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0ca844e8ca74eb781648.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0ca844e8ca74eb781648.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"235\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/0ca844e8ca74eb781648.webp", "assets/library-inline/21d93b3ab965cb9a8ae5.webp", "assets/library-inline/24c5c46bd555285ce849.webp", "assets/library-inline/2b9d5d3697a1c5b78fac.webp", "assets/library-inline/5752c3d76b5de2bde860.webp", "assets/library-inline/57d26788ef5671addcd7.webp", "assets/library-inline/6f8ce5e2801952d1e7a1.webp", "assets/library-inline/876a55550f1e433e45ed.webp", "assets/library-inline/8ef6175065ebd125b379.webp", "assets/library-inline/a3262e9b88112c02ccd2.webp", "assets/library-inline/b5c1cd1c7449e27ceced.webp", "assets/library-inline/d288d5b2ef5fe2f76061.webp", "assets/library-inline/e69f9be5689b67d2c1d0.webp", "assets/library-inline/ffc6f6067d8a4ec1279b.webp"];
